# Sets answer the same two method spellings lists do: `.append` adds an
# element unless the set already holds it, and `.depend` removes and returns
# one. Nothing here depends on iteration order (the engines promise none), so
# the printed values are lengths and a one-element pop.
#
# The set arm of these methods is what tools/check_fray_txt.py's audit of the
# reference turned up: the native emitter lowered every `.depend` to the list
# operation, so a set threw "IndexError: pop from empty list".

s = {5}
s.append(5)         # already present: deduplicated
print(len(s))       # 1
print(s.depend())   # 5
print(len(s))       # 0

s.append(7)
s.append(7)
s.append(8)
print(len(s))       # 2
s.depend()
print(len(s))       # 1
